Independently query each original Makefile's TEST_PROGS, TEST_GEN_PROGS and
TEST_FILES via a stdin-only print target. Twenty controls select:

collection                    arm64 aarch64 x86  x86+cross ppc64le
kexec                         empty empty   3*   3*        2
prctl                         empty empty   5    empty     empty
thermal/intel/power_floor      empty empty   1    empty     empty
thermal/intel/workload_hint    empty empty   1    empty     empty

* kexec's Makefile.arch tests CC's __LP64__; the sealed 64-bit gcc control
  enables the third x86-only jump program. This is make-variable inspection,
  NOT cross-compilation or execution of x86/ppc64le programs on ARM64.

kexec gates its entire body on ARCH_PROCESSED in {x86, ppc64le}; changing
kernel CONFIG_KEXEC cannot make this frozen arm64 recipe define its targets.
The other three first exclude CROSS_COMPILE, then require ARCH=x86. prctl
contains portable-looking individual sources too, but the original collection
does not admit them for this target. This records a frozen build-recipe limit,
not a claim that every prctl API is fundamentally x86-only. The two thermal
collections are explicitly Intel platform interfaces, not missing EvoCC C
language features. Enabling their guards would be a different test baseline.

Both arm64 spellings are empty. Native x86 selection enables the original
program lists; CROSS_COMPILE suppresses only the three guarded collections.
Original/staged Makefile bytes match. All sources are mounted read-only;
no binaries, kernel Images or disk copies are created. K12a classification
closes; the four buildExit=2 records and frozen 970-program population stay
unchanged. K12's configuration build/runtime work and K13 remain OPEN.

